    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 30 tests, 0 failures.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline already had a per-city capacity guard, so I followed that exact pattern for the new daily limit:\n\n1. **`EnsureDailyCapacity`** (new function) \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today; throws `DailyLimitException` when the count is \u2265 100. It runs *before* `BuildOwner`, so the count excludes the owner being created \u2014 the 101st registration today is the first rejected.\n\n2. **`DailyLimitException`** (new checked exception) \u2014 so it surfaces in the `throws` clause and routes through OfficeFloor escalation.\n\n3. **`DailyLimitExceptionHandler`** (new handler) \u2014 responds **429 Too Many Requests** with body `{\"errors\":[\"dailyLimit\"]}`.\n\n4. **`\u2026DailyLimitException.yml`** (new escalation wiring) \u2014 maps the exception to the handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureDailyCapacity` step (governed by the shared `transaction`) between `ensureCityCapacity` and `build`.\n\nThe new `Cp19Tests` (create 100 today, 101st \u2192 429) passes, and `Cp07Tests` (which asserts `registrationDate` defaulting/override behavior) and all other checkpoints remain green.",
